) VALUES (
  'alexandria-day-trip-from-cairo',
  'Alexandria Day Trip from Cairo: The Honest Logistics',
  '<p>Leave at nine and you will see two things badly.</p>

<p>That is the whole calculation for an alexandria day trip from cairo, and everything else follows from it. The drive is two and a half to three hours each way. The city''s sites are spread along fifteen kilometres of coast. If you are not moving before seven in the morning you are buying six hours of road for four hours of Alexandria, and you will spend most of those four hours in traffic between things.</p>

<p>Done properly it is one of the best days you can have out of Cairo. Done badly it is the day people describe afterwards as long.</p>

<h2>Train or Car</h2>

<p>The train is the better option for most people and almost nobody takes it.</p>

<p>Trains run from Ramses station to Misr station in central Alexandria in roughly two and a half hours. The express services are comfortable, they are not expensive, and they bypass the Desert Road entirely, which on a bad morning can add ninety minutes to a car journey. Book a day or two ahead for the faster departures.</p>

<p>The catch is what happens at the other end. Alexandria''s sites are strung along the corniche and you will need taxis between them, which means negotiating four or five times across the day.</p>

<p>A car and driver costs more and solves that completely. One vehicle, waiting at each stop, no negotiation, and you can reorder the day when something is closed. For two or more people the price difference narrows quickly.</p>

<h2>The Alexandria Day Trip from Cairo That Works</h2>

<p>Out of Cairo by 6:30 or 7. On the ground in Alexandria between 9 and 9:30. Leave the city by 4:30 or 5 and you are home for dinner.</p>

<p>Build the alexandria day trip from cairo around those two times and the middle sorts itself out. Miss the first one and nothing you do later in the day recovers it.</p>

<p>That gives you six to seven hours, and six to seven hours is four sites plus lunch. Not six sites. Four.</p>

<p>Anyone selling you a day trip to alexandria from cairo with seven stops on it is selling you a coach window. Ask how many hours are on the ground before you ask what is included.</p>

<h2>The Four Sites Worth Choosing</h2>

<p>These four make a coherent day and they sit in a workable line along the coast.</p>

<p><strong>The Catacombs of Kom el Shoqafa.</strong> A Roman tomb complex cut three levels into the rock, discovered in 1900 when a donkey fell into it. The decoration is the reason to come: Egyptian gods carved in Roman dress, Medusa over a doorway, a fusion you get nowhere else. The lowest level is usually flooded.</p>

<p><strong>The Roman Theatre at Kom el Dikka.</strong> Thirteen marble tiers, a set of Roman baths, lecture halls from a late antique school, and a villa with a floor of mosaic birds. It is central, compact and takes forty minutes.</p>

<p><strong>The Bibliotheca Alexandrina.</strong> The modern library, opened in 2002, a disc tilted toward the sea with the world''s alphabets carved into its granite skin. The reading room is eleven cascading levels under a slanted roof, and it is the best piece of contemporary architecture in Egypt.</p>

<p><strong>Qaitbay Citadel.</strong> A fifteenth century fort on the spit where the Pharos lighthouse stood until earthquakes brought it down. Some of its stone is in the walls. The interior is modest; the position, with the sea on three sides, is not.</p>

<h2>What to See in Alexandria Egypt if You Have Longer</h2>

<p>Pompey''s Pillar, a single twenty seven metre granite column with nothing around it, takes fifteen minutes and is next to the catacombs, which is the only reason to include it.</p>

<p>The Alexandria National Museum is well arranged, air conditioned and quiet, and it is the right call in bad weather. The Greco Roman Museum reopened after a long closure and holds the better collection of the two.</p>

<p>Montazah Palace gardens are at the far eastern end of the corniche, which puts them forty minutes from the catacombs in traffic. Lovely, and not compatible with a day trip.</p>

<h2>Lunch, and Why It Matters Here</h2>

<p>Alexandria is a seafood city and this is not a detail to skip for the sake of one more monument.</p>

<p>The fish restaurants around the Eastern Harbour work the same way: you choose from ice at the front, it is weighed, and it comes grilled with salads and bread. Ask the price per kilo before you point, because that is where the confusion always happens.</p>

<p>Budget an hour and put it between the theatre and the citadel, around one o''clock, which is when the light on the corniche is at its worst for photographs anyway.</p>

<h2>When Not to Do This as a Day Trip</h2>

<p>If you cannot leave before seven, do not go. You will arrive after eleven, see two sites in a rush and turn around.</p>

<p>If it is winter and the forecast is rain, reconsider. Alexandria gets real Mediterranean weather between December and March, the corniche is exposed, and the catacombs are worse in the wet.</p>

<p>And if you have any flexibility at all, stay the night. One night turns the same drive into two relaxed half days, gets you the corniche in the evening when the city is at its best, and lets you eat dinner rather than lunch at the harbour. The cost of a hotel is usually less than the cost of a second car day later in the trip.</p>

<h2>What the City Is Actually Like</h2>

<p>Worth saying, because expectations cause most of the disappointment here. Almost nothing of ancient Alexandria is visible. The Ptolemaic city is under the modern one, the lighthouse is in the harbour in pieces, and the ancient library is a story rather than a site.</p>

<p>What you get instead is a Mediterranean port with faded early twentieth century architecture, a long sea wall, tram lines, and a distinctly different feel from anywhere else in Egypt. People come expecting Cleopatra and find a city that is more Naples than Luxor.</p>

<p>If that sounds like a disappointment it is not. It is just a different day from the one the brochures imply, and knowing that in advance is most of the battle.</p>

<p>The other thing people underestimate is the sea. Cairo is three hours from a coast that most visitors never see, and standing on the corniche with the Mediterranean in front of you is a genuine change of register after a week of desert and river. Several people have told us it was their favourite hour in Egypt, which is not what anyone expects to hear about a day trip.</p>

<h2>Practical Notes</h2>

<p>Entry prices at the Alexandria sites change most years and are card only at the major ones. Check the current rate before you go, or ask your operator to confirm it with your itinerary. The Bibliotheca charges separately for its internal museums.</p>

<p>Photography is allowed inside the catacombs with a phone and restricted with anything larger. This rule changes; ask at the window.</p>

<p>Take a light layer even in summer. Alexandria runs several degrees cooler than Cairo and the wind off the harbour at Qaitbay is steady enough to feel cold in a shirt that was fine at the catacombs an hour earlier.</p>

<p>The corniche road is the main artery and it is slow in the middle of the day. If your driver suggests a parallel inland route between two sites, let him.</p>

<p>For the wider picture, the <a href="/egypt-travel-guide/alexandria-egypt-attractions">Alexandria destination guide</a> covers the sites in detail and the <a href="/egypt-travel-guide/cairo-travel-guide">Cairo guide</a> covers what you are leaving behind for the day. If the day trip from cairo to alexandria is competing with a second Cairo day in your plan, the <a href="/blog/grand-egyptian-museum-tour">Grand Egyptian Museum guide</a> is the comparison worth reading first.</p>

<p>Our <a href="/egypt-private-tours">private day trips</a> run this as a car and driver with a fixed departure time rather than a coach, mostly because the departure time is the part that decides whether the day works.</p>

<p>One last thing. Sit on the left side of the train going up and the right coming back. You get the farmland and the canals rather than the desert, and on the return leg you get the sunset over the Delta, which is a better use of three hours than a phone screen.</p>',
  'Three hours each way leaves about six on the ground, which is enough for four sites if you choose them before you leave. Here is the version that works and the version that does not.',
